// A CO-TENANT THAT ERASES ANOTHER CO-TENANT'S TYPE. No fixture in this package
// had that shape, and the two that come closest are each blind to exactly this
// arm.
//
// `testdata/existential/` puts the interface, the implementors and the boxing
// site in three files that form no cycle, so they are three builder
// PACKAGES and every crossing takes the qualified spelling. `testdata/
// cycle_merge/` forms a real cycle and therefore one merged package, but
// nothing in it is interface-TYPED: its `Show` is only ever dispatched on a
// value the calling file built itself, so no mirror of it is ever a component
// of another type.
//
// This fixture is both at once: a three-file import cycle — so all three land
// in ONE builder package (component.go) — in which
//
//   - `App.logger` is typed by an interface a CO-TENANT declares, so mirroring
//     `App` walks `importKind`'s `tagIface` arm, which refused outright
//     (`existential over a sibling file type`);
//   - `wire.loud()` boxes wire's OWN `Prod`, so the identity variable is local
//     to the boxing gen;
//   - `wire.hushed()` boxes `extra.Quiet` — a THIRD co-tenant's type — so
//     `tidRef` takes its mirror path with `o.pkg == g.pkg` and must spell the
//     owner's variable BARE. Qualifying it there is a Go self-import; minting a
//     second one splits one type into two identities.
//
// # The failure is SILENT, which is why the answers below are what they are
//
// An `rt.TypeID` is what dispatch keys on. Get it wrong and nothing fails to
// compile: `rt.Method` misses, `Display` renders the anonymous form, `==`
// degrades to structural comparison and answers False, ordering traps. So this
// fixture READS a dispatch table twice with two different implementors behind
// one interface-typed field — `P>ready` against `q3:ready` — and the two
// answers differ in every character. A collapsed identity prints one of them
// where the other belongs, which the differential harness catches; a table that
// was never bound prints neither.
import {
    extra
    wire.{self, Tagger}
}

pub interface Logger {
    fn log(value: self, message: String): String
}

// TWO interface-typed fields, and the second one is not redundancy — it is the
// CROSSING-BACK arm.
//
//	`logger: Logger`  declared HERE. A co-tenant mirroring `App` resolves it to
//	                  a mirror of this file's declaration.
//	`tag: Tagger`     declared in WIRE. When wire mirrors `App` the component
//	                  points back at wire's OWN declaration, so the answer must
//	                  be wire's local def and NOT a mirror of it — a mirror
//	                  there is a second identity for one interface, and the
//	                  symptom is a dispatch table nothing bound. importNamed
//	                  carries the same arm for a type; without it in
//	                  importIfaceKind a mutation is invisible from output.
pub struct App {
    name: String
    logger: Logger
    tag: Tagger
}

// Dispatch on TWO erased receivers, one per interface. `Logger`'s table is this
// package's own variable; `Tagger`'s belongs to wire, which shares this
// builder package after the merge — so it must be spelled BARE.
pub fn banner(a: App): String {
    "${a.name}[${Tagger.tag(a.tag)}]: ${Logger.log(a.logger, "ready")}"
}

// Closes the cycle in this direction: core names both siblings, and each names
// core back. Without an edge FROM core the three files are not one strongly
// connected component and the package would not merge — which would make this
// fixture a slower copy of testdata/existential.
pub fn origin(): String {
    "${wire.tag}|${extra.tag}"
}
